## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to perform multi-word arithmetic with pure bitwise/arith operations, handling carry without branches.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit arithmetic via two 64-bit halves
- Carry propagation using unsigned overflow properties
- Branchless computation

## Code Example
```c
void add128(uint64_t a_hi, uint64_t a_lo, uint64_t b_hi, uint64_t b_lo, uint64_t *out_hi, uint64_t *out_lo){
  uint64_t lo = a_lo + b_lo;
  uint64_t carry = (lo < a_lo);
  uint64_t hi = a_hi + b_hi + carry;
  *out_hi = hi;
  *out_lo = lo;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this to 256-bit operands?
- What are the potential pitfalls on platforms with different endianness?

## Why This Is Asked
Demonstrates mastery of fixed-popcount permutations. Tests carry propagation and ability to explain a one-shot solution without loops.

## Key Concepts
- Snoob algorithm for next with same popcount
- Carry ripple and bitwise masks
- Edge-case handling for zero or maximal permutations

## Code Example
```c
static inline uint64_t nextBitPermutation64(uint64_t x) {
    // Assumes x != 0 and not the maximal permutation for its popcount
    uint64_t smallest = x & -x;
    uint64_t ripple = x + smallest;
    uint64_t ones = x ^ ripple;
    ones = (ones >> 2) / smallest;
    return ripple | ones;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to arbitrary bit-widths?
- Compare this approach to a loop-based generator in terms of performance and readability.

## Why This Is Asked

- Tests ability to implement a canonical bit-counting trick without loops, emphasizing constant-time operations. 
- Demonstrates careful use of masks and shifts to aggregate counts across bit groups.

## Key Concepts

- SWAR (parallel bit counting)
- Bit masks 0x55555555, 0x33333333, 0x0F0F0F0F
- Constant-time arithmetic with shifts

## Code Example

```javascript
function popcount32(x) {
  x = x - ((x >>> 1) & 0x55555555);
  x = (x & 0x33333333) + ((x >>> 2) & 0x33333333);
  x = (x + (x >>> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F;
  return (x * 0x01010101) >>> 24;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt to 64-bit inputs?
- Compare this approach to using hardware popcount instructions in terms of portability and performance.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of in-place bitboard style transforms, pure bitwise logic, and branchless design for a fixed-width data layout.

## Key Concepts
- Bit-slice permutation across 8x8 matrix
- Masked swaps and shifts to perform transposition in O(1)
- Endianness and layout nullified by a consistent row-major interpretation

## Code Example
```javascript
function transpose8x8(x) {
  // Use BigInt for 64-bit arithmetic in JS
  let t;
  const m1 = 0x5555555555555555n;
  t = (x ^ (x >> 1n)) & m1;
  x = x ^ t ^ (t << 1n);

  const m2 = 0x3333333333333333n;
  t = (x ^ (x >> 2n)) & m2;
  x = x ^ t ^ (t << 2n);

  const m4 = 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0Fn;
  t = (x ^ (x >> 4n)) & m4;
  x = x ^ t ^ (t << 4n);

  return x;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to 16x16 on a 128-bit word? needed masks and shifts?
- How would you handle different input mappings (column-major) or endianness concerns?

