## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about multi-word bit manipulations and safe 64→128-bit extension without loops.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit reversal via two 64-bit reversals and swapping halves
- Parallel-bits reversal (mask/shifts)
- Endianness-insensitive mapping of bit indices

## Code Example
```javascript
function reverse64(x) {
  x = (x >> 1n) & 0x5555555555555555n | (x & 0x5555555555555555n) << 1n;
  x = (x >> 2n) & 0x3333333333333333n | (x & 0x3333333333333333n) << 2n;
  x = (x >> 4n) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0Fn | (x & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0Fn) << 4n;
  x = (x >> 8n) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFn | (x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFn) << 8n;
  x = (x >> 16n) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFn | (x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFn) << 16n;
  x = (x >> 32n) | (x << 32n);
  return x;
}
function reverse128(hi, lo) {
  const revLo = reverse64(lo);
  const revHi = reverse64(hi);
  return { hi: revLo, lo: revHi };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to streaming 128-bit values?
- How to verify correctness with random tests?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to implement a branchless popcount for 128-bit data using only bitwise ops, a core skill in performance-critical code paths like fingerprinting or anomaly detection.

## Key Concepts

- Branchless bit counting (SWAR)
- Apply per-64-bit halves independently
- Combine results without overflow or branches

## Code Example

```javascript
// Conceptual: 64-bit helper (C-like pseudocode)
unsigned int popcount64(unsigned long long x){
  x -= (x>>1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL;
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x>>2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x>>4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  return (unsigned int)((x * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56);
}
unsigned int popcount128(uint128 x){
  return popcount64(x.hi) + popcount64(x.lo);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt if you needed to accumulate counts in a streaming fashion?
- How does this interact with endianness in cross-language bindings?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests cross-word bit ops and branchless parity reduction. It exercises combining two 64-bit halves and a compact reduction, scalable to wider architectures.

## Key Concepts

- Parity via XOR folding
- 128-bit composition hi^lo
- Branchless reductions

## Code Example

```c
int parity128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo) {
  uint64_t x = hi ^ lo;
  x ^= x >> 32;
  x ^= x >> 16;
  x ^= x >> 8;
  x ^= x >> 4;
  return x & 1;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend to a 256-bit value?  - Can you adapt this for streaming parity on large data chunks?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests understanding of fixed-depth carry logic and 128-bit arithmetic without loops—critical in high-throughput code paths.

## Key Concepts

- **Carry-lookahead across 4×32-bit lanes**: Break 128 bits into four 32-bit chunks for parallel carry computation.
- **Propagate (P) and Generate (G) signals**: P = a ^ b (carry passes through), G = a & b (carry generated).
- **Fixed-depth carry computation**: Uses only OR/AND operations, achieving O(1) depth.
- **Correct 128-bit carry propagation**: Ensure carries propagate correctly across all four lanes.

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 add128(uint128 a, uint128 b) {
    uint64_t a0 = (uint32_t)a.lo;
    uint64_t a1 = (uint32_t)(a.lo >> 32);
    uint64_t a2 = (uint32_t)a.hi;
    uint64_t a3 = (uint32_t)(a.hi >> 32);

    uint64_t b0 = (uint32_t)b.lo;
    uint64_t b1 = (uint32_t)(b.lo >> 32);
    uint64_t b2 = (uint32_t)b.hi;
    uint64_t b3 = (uint32_t)(b.hi >> 32);

    uint64_t p0 = a0 ^ b0, g0 = a0 & b0;
    uint64_t p1 = a1 ^ b1, g1 = a1 & b1;
    uint64_t p2 = a2 ^ b2, g2 = a2 & b2;
    uint64_t p3 = a3 ^ b3, g3 = a3 & b3;

    uint64_t c1 = g0;
    uint64_t c2 = g0 | (p0 & g1);
    uint64_t c3 = g0 | (p0 & g1) | (p0 & p1 & g2);
    uint64_t c4 = g0 | (p0 & g1) | (p0 & p1 & g2) | (p0 & p1 & p2 & g3);

    uint128 r;
    r.lo = (p3 ^ c4) | ((p2 ^ c3) << 32) | ((p1 ^ c2) << 64) | ((p0 ^ c1) << 96);
    r.hi = (p3 ^ c4) >> 32;
    return r;
}
```
